# One model key is required. Whichever is present wins; MODEL_PROVIDER forces
# a choice when both are set. Without either, cards still render from the
# corpus — only the written prose is missing.

GEMINI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- optional ---
# MODEL_PROVIDER=gemini              # or: anthropic

# Gemini. The code default is gemini-3.6-flash; set this to pin another.
# A key without quota for the pinned model returns 429 and the card renders
# with no prose, so check /api/health after changing it.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.6-flash
# GEMINI_THINKING_BUDGET=0           # 2.x only. -1 for dynamic thinking; 0 keeps TTFT low
# GEMINI_THINKING_LEVEL=MINIMAL      # 3.x only. MINIMAL | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH

# The system prompt is ~7,600 tokens and identical on every request. Cached, it
# is billed once and read back at a fraction; uncached it is the largest line
# on the bill and buys nothing. On by default, and every failure path falls
# back to sending it inline — a lost cache costs money, never an answer.
# Watch the `[tokens]` log lines: `cached` against `prompt` is the hit rate.
# GEMINI_CACHE=off                   # disable entirely; prompt goes inline every turn
# GEMINI_CACHE_TTL=3600              # seconds the cache is held; renewed before expiry

# Anthropic.
# RESTORATION_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# RESTORATION_EFFORT=low             # low | medium | high | xhigh | max

# Absolute URL for OG image resolution in production.
# SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Meta Pixel. Unset means off, which is what a local run and CI both want —
# with an ID here, every dev machine and preview deploy reports into the same
# ad account as production and the campaign optimises against your own testing.
# Numeric dataset ID only; anything else is rejected rather than injected.
# Set it in the production environment, not in a committed file.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=

# Campaign links should carry their own labels, since the pixel cannot tell you
# which placement a visitor came from and the first-party log can:
#   https://example.com/?utm_source=instagram&utm_medium=paid&utm_campaign=diwali
# Printed QR codes have no referrer, so mark them explicitly and `qr_entry`
# fires: https://example.com/?utm_source=qr&utm_campaign=packaging

# Vector fallback to the pipeline index. ON by default: it answers property
# queries BM25 cannot. Semantic matches are offered to the model as candidates
# and only promoted on a RESTORE verdict. See src/lib/corpus/load.ts.
# VECTOR_FALLBACK=off   # disable the index fallback; keyword-only over the 31 records

# The fallback reaches the pipeline index, so the app needs the same Pinecone +
# Gemini-embedding keys pipeline/.env carries. These are the retrieval keys, not
# the model keys above:
# PINECONE_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY is reused for query embeddings (gemini-embedding-001).

# Reranking. The second retrieval stage (cross-encoder over the dense shortlist)
# picks a vendor by key presence, DeepInfra first — it is the only one with no
# monthly request cap. Without a key here the app falls through to Pinecone's
# hosted bge reranker, which is capped at 500 requests/month org-wide and, once
# exhausted, returns 429 and silently degrades retrieval to dense order. Mirror
# the value from pipeline/.env. See pipeline/lib/rerank.ts.
# DEEPINFRA_API_KEY=
# RERANK_PROVIDER=deepinfra   # force a vendor: deepinfra | jina | cohere | pinecone | none
# JINA_API_KEY=               # alternative rerank vendors, if not using DeepInfra
# COHERE_API_KEY=

# Conversation mirror. Threads are written to the device first and copied here
# second, so without this the app behaves exactly as it did before: history
# lives in localStorage and nothing is lost, it just does not survive the
# browser forgetting it. Use the POOLED connection string — route handlers run
# per request and would otherwise open a connection each time.
# Run `npm run db:migrate` once to create the table.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Launch email tracking -------------------------------------------------
# The redirect tracker at /r, the open pixel at /px.gif and /unsubscribe all
# write through the same DATABASE_URL above. Without it they still work — the
# reader is redirected, the pixel is served, the page renders — and nothing is
# recorded. That is deliberate: a tracker is never allowed to break a CTA.
# Run `npm run db:migrate` once to create email_events and email_suppressions.

# Salt for the click fingerprint, which is sha256(salt|ip|user-agent) stored
# instead of the address so the events table is not a table of people.
#
# Optional. Unset, the salt is derived from the database connection string —
# already in the environment, already secret, stable for the life of the
# deployment. Set this explicitly only if the connection string might be rotated
# mid-campaign, since the salt would change with it and the same reader would
# then count as two people.
# TRACK_SALT=

# SITE_URL is read above for OG images and is also the origin every on-site
# redirect destination is built from. It is taken from the environment rather
# than from the request's Host header on purpose: building a redirect out of a
# client-controlled header is an open redirect by a quieter route. Unset, /r
# sends people to https://kranticookbook.com — set SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# to exercise the tracker locally.

# The email links three codes: ai (Discover the Kranti Cookbook AI), film (the
# YouTube film) and post (the Instagram account). They are defined in
# src/lib/email/destinations.ts, never in a query string.

# Reading the numbers without a connection string.
#
# The Neon credential on this deployment was created by Vercel's integration and
# may be stored write-only, so the people running the campaign can deploy the
# site but cannot read the database it is already talking to. Set this to any
# long random string and the numbers are readable at
#   /api/email-report?token=<this>&sent=<how many you sent>[&tokens=1]
# It is a NEW variable you choose yourself, so it can be created without being
# able to read anything that already exists. Unset, the route 404s — it fails
# closed, because a report URL that serves everybody is worse than none.
# EMAIL_REPORT_TOKEN=

# --- The kitchen (analytics dashboard at /kitchen) -------------------------
# One shared password, chosen by you. Unset, /kitchen 404s and the auth
# endpoint does too — it fails closed, because a dashboard that serves
# everybody because somebody forgot a variable is worse than no dashboard.
#
# There are no accounts. The password is compared in constant time and never
# echoed into the cookie; the session is a signed token with a twelve-hour
# life. Changing this password invalidates every live session, which is what
# you want the day somebody leaves.
KITCHEN_PASSWORD=

# Optional. The session signing key, which is otherwise derived from the
# password above. Set it only if you want sessions to survive a password
# change — most deployments should leave it unset.
# KITCHEN_SECRET=

ATLAS_URL=
ATLAS_PASSWORD=
ATLAS_USER=

ADMIN_PASSWORD=